{"id":"79ec6bf2d6d5a6ff28d2d9c3fcd9a9f1b7af100d","axis":"silent","repo":"WordPress/gutenberg","sha":"79ec6bf2d6d5a6ff28d2d9c3fcd9a9f1b7af100d","parsed":{"files":[{"path":"docs/reference-guides/interactivity-api/directives-and-store.md","lines":["@@ -402,7 +402,7 @@ On the same element as `data-wp-text`, `data-wp-each` or `data-wp-router-region`"," -   **Trusted Types.**","     -   The package creates no policy. Pass `asDangerousHTML( policy.createHTML( markup ) )`.","     -   A rejected value empties the element, and a warning is shown in development mode.","-    -   Known limitation: elements created after hydration, such as `data-wp-each` items, whose value is pending or `{ \"wpServerHtml\": true }` must have an empty fallback on sites enforcing Trusted Types.","+    -   The fallback of an element the client creates, such as a [`data-wp-each`](#wp-each) item or an element added by client-side navigation, is copied into the element as nodes, not as a string, so it never goes through a Trusted Types policy, default policy included. A default policy that transforms, filters or rejects HTML doesn't apply to it, and the markup appears as the server rendered it. Don't rely on a policy to prepare fallback markup: it isn't sanitized, apart from leaving out `<script>` elements, so write it as you want it shown, from content you trust. This doesn't change values from `asDangerousHTML()`, which the browser still checks when they are rendered."," -   **\"Inert\" means inert to the Interactivity API only.** The inserted content is still live browser HTML. Content that isn't safe to render as HTML isn't made safe by going through `wp-html`."," "," ### `wp-on`"],"additions":1,"deletions":1,"truncated":false},{"path":"packages/e2e-tests/plugins/interactive-blocks/directive-html/render.php","lines":["@@ -8,9 +8,38 @@"," wp_interactivity_state("," \t'directive-html',"," \tarray(","-\t\t'serverHtml' => array( 'wpServerHtml' => true ),","+\t\t'serverHtml'           => array( 'wpServe